# Working on Porcupine

Instructions for any AI assistant working in this repository — Gemini, Claude,
or anything else. Written so a second assistant produces work the first one
would recognise.

Read `AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md` in `apps/web` too. **Next.js here is version
16.3 and differs from most training data** — check `node_modules/next/dist/docs/`
before writing App Router code rather than assuming.

---

## The stack, briefly

pnpm 11 workspace. Next 16.3 App Router + React 19.2. TypeScript 5.9 in strict
mode with `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` and `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` — both
bite, and both are deliberate. Prisma 7 for writes, supabase-js for reads (the
JWT travels with the request so PostgREST evaluates RLS against it). Postgres
17 via Supabase. Tailwind 4. Zod 4. Vitest + Playwright + pgTAP.

## Rules that are not negotiable

**RLS is the security boundary, not the application.** Every table has
`FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`. The app connects as `porcupine_app`, which does not
have BYPASSRLS. Views are `security_invoker`. Never add a `WHERE user_id = ...`
in place of a policy — if a query needs one to be safe, the policy is wrong.

**Every pgTAP `count(*) = 0` needs a mutation check.** Pair it with the same
query as `postgres`, which must return rows. Without that, a test asserting
"the attacker sees nothing" passes just as happily against an empty table.
Scope those checks to the fixture's own project id — counting the whole table
made them fail the moment the app wrote real data.

**A rule is not done until it has been seen to fail.** Break it on purpose,
watch the test go red, put it back. Tests written against working code prove
only that the code runs.

**Absent and forbidden are the same answer.** RLS returns nothing for a project
you are not a member of, so pages return 404 rather than 403. Distinguishing
them confirms the project exists.

**Say what is true.** If a number is estimated, say so. If a feature is not
built, write that on the screen rather than shipping a control that does
nothing. Several rounds of work in this repo have been spent finding
capabilities that were documented, typed, and never wired to anything —
`structureUpgradePath`, `workInputSchema`, the Master Key, member removal, the
`questions` table. When you add a field or a flag, add the thing that writes it
in the same change.

## Style

Match the surrounding code — it has a voice and consistency matters more than
your preference. In particular:

- **Comments explain WHY, and name what was tried and rejected.** This codebase
  is full of comments recording a wrong turn so the next person does not take
  it again. That is the house style; keep it. Do not write comments that
  restate the code.
- Prose in comments and UI copy: plain, direct, no marketing. Contractions are
  fine. Em dashes are used heavily.
- UI copy names the WORK, not the screen. "Continue screening", not "Go to
  screening page".
- Accessibility is a launch requirement, not polish — university procurement
  disqualifies inaccessible tools. Every interactive element gets a 44px touch
  target and a real accessible name. axe runs on two viewports in CI.

## Before you push

```bash
pnpm verify            # lint, typecheck, unit, pgTAP, RLS suite
pnpm verify --e2e      # adds build + Playwright; needed for any UI change
```

**Run the full suite ONCE when the work is done, not after every edit.** Use
`pnpm exec tsc --noEmit` and a single spec file while iterating; a full
Playwright run is minutes and burning them one at a time is the main way time
disappears here.

Useful narrower commands:

```bash
cd apps/web && pnpm exec tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json
cd apps/web && E2E_PORT=3100 pnpm exec playwright test e2e/one.spec.ts --project=chromium
cd packages/db && node scripts/pgtap.mjs
```

`pnpm db:generate` is NOT part of `pnpm verify`. Run it after any schema
change or the Prisma client is silently stale.

## Playwright traps this repo has already hit

- `count()` and `isVisible()` do **not** auto-wait. Use `expect(locator)` or
  `waitFor({ state: "visible" })`.
- `getByText` matches hidden nodes, including React's `<div hidden id="S:0">`
  streaming slot. Navigate with the `goto` helper in `e2e/ready.ts`, which
  waits for that slot to be reclaimed. Prefer role queries, which skip it.
- `getByRole("alert")` also matches Next's empty route announcer.
- Do not share an account across Playwright projects — scope emails with
  `testInfo.project.name`.
- Enrolment runs Argon2id and is slow. Hooks that enrol need
  `test.setTimeout(180_000)`.

## Git

**Branch and push. Do not open a pull request** unless asked, or unless the
change genuinely needs review. The user works alone on this and a PR is
ceremony.

```bash
git checkout -b some-descriptive-branch
# ... work, verify ...
git push -u origin some-descriptive-branch
```

Merge to `main` only when asked. Use `--no-ff` for a phase boundary so the
shape of the work stays visible in the history.

**Commit messages carry the reasoning.** Subject line in the imperative, then
prose explaining what was wrong and why this is the fix — not a list of files.
If a change corrects an earlier mistake, say what the mistake was. Look at
`git log` before writing one; the standard is high and consistent.

End every commit message with:

```
Co-Authored-By: <your model name> <noreply@anthropic.com>
```

Commit incrementally — the user is often away and expects to find progress
rather than one enormous commit.

## Working with this user

- They will ask for several things at once. Do all of them, or say plainly
  which you did not and why. Do not silently narrow scope.
- They prefer decisions made for them: "you take the best decision for the best
  optimisation of the app". Recommend, then act — do not present a menu.
- Report failures honestly, with the output. Never claim a test passed that you
  did not run.
- If something they ask for is a bad idea, say so in a sentence or two and then
  build the rest. If they repeat the request, it is their call — except where
  the objection was legal or ethical rather than technical.

## Where things are

```
apps/web/src/app          routes (App Router)
apps/web/src/components   shared UI + primitives in ui.tsx
apps/web/src/lib          client helpers, crypto session, project sections
apps/web/e2e              Playwright specs; ready.ts has the goto helper
packages/db               Prisma schema, seed, pgTAP suite in test/
packages/crypto           libsodium wrappers: identity, project keys, messages
packages/shared           screening vocabulary, capabilities, protocol templates
packages/discovery        the five bibliographic providers + SSRF guards
supabase/migrations       SQL, applied in filename order
docs/                     numbered build plans; BUILD-LOG.md records decisions
```

`apps/web/src/lib/project-sections.ts` is the single source of truth for which
screens a project kind has. Add a section there, not in the nav components.
